  "topic": "school life",
  "words": 313,
  "passage": "Every Wednesday morning at Hoa Sen Secondary School begins in an unusual way. There is no bell, no assembly, and no teacher standing at the front of the room. Instead, for exactly sixty minutes, the whole school reads. Students may choose any book they like, sit anywhere on the school grounds, and nobody is allowed to ask them a question about what they have read.<br><br>The idea came from Ms Thu, the school librarian, who noticed that borrowing had dropped by almost half in three years. \"Students told me they had no time,\" she says. \"Between homework, extra classes and their phones, reading had simply fallen off the list.\" She suggested taking one hour from the timetable and giving it back to books. The head teacher agreed to a trial of one term.<br><br>At first, not everyone was pleased. Some parents worried that their children would lose an hour of maths or English. A few students admitted that they spent the first two sessions staring at the same page. But by the middle of the term, something had changed. The library was running out of popular titles, and students had started swapping books with each other in the corridors.<br><br>The school has also noticed some effects it did not expect. Teachers report that Wednesday classes are calmer, and several students who rarely spoke in lessons have joined a new book club that meets at lunchtime. \"I used to think reading was something you did for a test,\" says Minh, a grade 9 student. \"Now I read on the bus, in the queue, everywhere.\"<br><br>The trial has now been extended to the whole year. Ms Thu is careful not to call it a success yet. \"One hour a week won't fix everything,\" she says. \"But it has reminded a lot of people that reading can be a pleasure, and that was the whole point.\"",
