  "passage": "Imagine a football match in which nobody is allowed to run. The ball moves, the players move, but the moment anyone breaks into a jog, the referee blows the whistle and gives the ball to the other team. This is walking football, and it is one of the fastest-growing sports in Britain, though it is played mostly by people over fifty.<br><br>The game was invented in 2011 by a football club in the north of England that wanted its older supporters to stay active. Its rules are simple. Teams have five or six players, the pitch is small, the ball must stay below head height, and there is no physical contact. Matches last about twenty minutes each way, which sounds short until you have tried walking quickly for that long.<br><br>The sport's popularity has surprised even the people who started it. Today there are more than a thousand clubs across the country, and national tournaments attract hundreds of teams. Doctors have begun to recommend it to patients recovering from heart problems, because it raises the heart rate without the sudden stops and jumps that make ordinary football risky for older bodies.<br><br>For many players, however, the health benefits are only part of the story. \"I played every weekend until I was forty,\" says Ray, sixty-eight, who now captains a team in Manchester. \"Then my knees gave up and I thought that was the end. Walking football gave me my Saturday mornings back.\" Players often mention the friendships they have made, and clubs say that members who live alone are the ones who almost never miss a session.<br><br>Not everyone approves. Some critics argue that a game without running is not really football, and a few players have been known to bend the rules when the referee is not looking. But as Ray puts it, \"At my age, being told off for running too fast is a compliment.\"",
