  "topic": "animals",
  "words": 325,
  "passage": "Walk through a rainforest in Central or South America and you may notice a thin green line moving across the ground. Look closer and you will see thousands of ants, each carrying a piece of leaf many times larger than its own body. These are leaf-cutter ants, and although they spend their days collecting leaves, they never actually eat them.<br><br>Instead, the ants are farmers. Back in the nest, the leaves are chewed into a paste and used to grow a special fungus, which is the colony's only food. The fungus cannot survive outside the nest, and the ants cannot survive without the fungus. Scientists believe this partnership began more than fifty million years ago, long before humans planted their first crops.<br><br>A single colony can contain several million ants, and each has a job to do. The largest are soldiers, whose powerful jaws defend the nest. Medium-sized workers cut and carry the leaves. The smallest ants ride on top of the leaf pieces as they are carried home. For a long time, nobody understood why. Then researchers discovered that a type of fly attacks the workers while they walk, laying eggs on their heads. The tiny riders act as guards, chasing the flies away.<br><br>The ants are also careful gardeners. If a piece of leaf turns out to be harmful to the fungus, the workers stop collecting from that plant within a day or two. They remove diseased parts of the garden and carry waste to a separate area far from the growing fungus, in much the same way that human farmers keep a compost heap away from their fields.<br><br>Farmers in the region, of course, do not always see the ants as fellow farmers. A large colony can strip a fruit tree of its leaves overnight. Still, many scientists argue that we have a great deal to learn from an animal that solved the problems of agriculture millions of years before we did.",
