  "topic": "family",
  "words": 318,
  "passage": "When my parents both started working full-time, our house changed. Nobody knew who was picking up my little brother from football, whose turn it was to feed the dog, or why the fridge was empty again on Thursday. My mother's answer was a large paper calendar, stuck to the fridge door with four magnets, where every member of the family had to write their plans.<br><br>At first my brother and I thought it was silly. Ten-year-old Nam drew pictures instead of words, and I \"forgot\" to write anything at all because I did not want my parents to know how often I stayed late at my friend's house. But the calendar slowly did its job. When my father wrote \"Late meeting, back at 8\" on a Tuesday, my mother knew she would have to cook, and she asked me to help. When I wrote \"Maths test\" on a Friday, nobody asked me to wash the car that evening.<br><br>The most surprising result was that we started talking more, not less. Every Sunday evening, we stood in front of the fridge for ten minutes and planned the week. Those ten minutes often turned into an hour. My father told us about problems at his office; Nam explained the rules of a game he had invented; I admitted that I was worried about choosing a school. It was the first time I had heard my parents discuss money in front of us, and although it was a little frightening, it made me feel trusted.<br><br>Two years later, my mother has a calendar on her phone that sends everyone a message automatically. It is faster and it never runs out of space. Yet the paper one is still on the fridge, covered in Nam's drawings, and on Sunday evenings we still meet in the kitchen. The plans are on the phone now. The family, it turns out, is on the fridge.",
