  "title": "What a Week of Cooking Taught Me",
  "topic": "food",
  "words": 347,
  "passage": "Last month our science teacher gave the class an unusual homework task: for one week, each of us had to cook one meal a day for our family and keep a diary. Most of my classmates groaned. I was quietly pleased, because I had always watched my grandmother in the kitchen and believed I knew what I was doing.<br><br>I was wrong. On the first evening I decided to make fried rice, which looked simple in every video I had seen. I put the rice in the pan while it was still hot from the cooker, added too much oil, and turned the heat up as high as possible. The result was a pale, sticky mess that my father ate politely and my sister refused to touch. My grandmother laughed and told me the secret: fried rice needs rice that was cooked yesterday and has dried out in the fridge. Fresh rice is too wet.<br><br>Each day after that I learned one more thing that videos never mention. A knife should be sharp, not because it cuts faster but because a blunt one slips. Salt added at the end tastes stronger than salt added at the start. Vegetables should go into the soup in the order of how long they take to soften, so the carrots before the leaves. None of this was complicated, but nobody had ever explained it to me, and I had never asked.<br><br>By Friday my sister ate everything on her plate. By Sunday my grandmother sat down at the table and let me serve her, which she had never done before. When I handed in my diary, I expected the teacher to talk about vitamins and energy, which we had studied in class. Instead she asked us one question: what had we learned about the people we cooked for? I wrote that my sister will eat anything if it is cut small enough, that my father says \"delicious\" before he has tasted it, and that my grandmother, who never sits still, will stop for a meal made by someone who has tried.",
