  "passage": "When the storm knocked out the internet in our building last month, my first feeling was panic. It was seven in the evening, I had homework to submit online, and my younger brother had already announced that his life was over. My mother, who grew up in a house with one telephone in the hallway, simply lit a candle and asked what we wanted to do.<br><br>The truth is that our home depends on the internet more than any of us had noticed. The lights in the living room are switched on with a phone. The fridge sends a message when the milk is finished. Even the front door has a small camera that shows visitors on our screens. Without the connection, the lights still worked, but only from the old switch on the wall, which my brother could not find at first. The fridge went silent. The door became a door again.<br><br>What surprised me was how quickly the evening filled up. My brother found a box of board games under his bed that he had not opened since he was nine. My father, who normally reads the news on his tablet after dinner, told us instead about the games he had played as a boy in a village with no electricity at all. I finished my homework on paper and photographed it the next morning, and my teacher accepted it without a word.<br><br>I am not going to pretend that we have given up technology. The internet came back at midnight, and by half past twelve my brother was online again. But the evening changed one thing. We now have a \"no screens\" hour after dinner on Wednesdays. It was my brother's idea, which shows how much a single evening can change a person. Modern homes are wonderful, and I would not want to live without the fridge that talks. It is just useful to know that, if it stops talking, we still know how to talk to each other.",
