  "title": "Slow Travel: Why Some Tourists Are Taking the Long Way",
  "topic": "travel",
  "words": 321,
  "passage": "When Linh's family planned their first trip to Da Lat, her father wanted to fly. A plane would take fifty minutes; the bus would take eight hours. In the end, they chose the bus, and Linh says it was the best decision of the holiday. \"On the plane you see clouds,\" she explains. \"On the bus we saw rice fields, waterfalls and a village where every house was painted blue.\"<br><br>Linh's family are part of a growing movement known as \"slow travel\". Instead of visiting as many places as possible in a week, slow travellers stay longer in one town, walk or cycle rather than take taxis, and eat where local people eat. The idea is simple: a holiday should be about experience, not about ticking off a list of famous sights.<br><br>Supporters of slow travel point to several advantages. It is often cheaper, because buses and trains cost less than flights and a week in a rented room costs less than seven nights in seven hotels. It is also better for the environment, since a single flight produces far more carbon dioxide per passenger than a train journey of the same distance. Perhaps most importantly, travellers say they return home less tired. \"A fast holiday feels like homework,\" one blogger wrote. \"A slow one feels like a rest.\"<br><br>Of course, slow travel is not for everyone. People with only three days off cannot spend two of them on a train. Families with very young children may find long journeys difficult. And some travellers simply enjoy the excitement of a new city every morning.<br><br>Still, the trend is spreading. Several Vietnamese travel companies now sell \"one town, one week\" packages, and the number of overnight train tickets sold between Ha Noi and Hue rose by nearly a third last year. Linh has already decided where her family will go next summer. \"Somewhere far,\" she says, \"and we are definitely not flying.\"",
