  "title": "What a Weather Forecast Really Promises",
  "topic": "weather",
  "words": 319,
  "passage": "Every evening, millions of people check tomorrow's weather before they go to bed. If the forecast says \"70% chance of rain\", most of them will pack an umbrella. But surprisingly few could explain what that number actually means, and the confusion leads to a lot of unfair complaints.<br><br>A forecast of \"70% chance of rain\" does not mean that it will rain for 70% of the day, or that 70% of the city will get wet. It means that, on days with conditions like tomorrow's, rain fell at your location seven times out of ten. Three times out of ten, it stayed dry. So if you wake up to blue sky, the forecaster was not wrong. The forecast simply described a possibility, and the less likely result happened.<br><br>Forecasters gather information from thousands of sources: weather stations on the ground, balloons that rise twenty kilometres into the sky, ships, aircraft and satellites. Powerful computers then use this information to calculate how the air will move over the next few days. The calculations are remarkably good for the first three days. After that, tiny differences in the starting information grow larger and larger, and by day ten the forecast is little better than a guess based on the season.<br><br>This is why a forecaster in Ho Chi Minh City can be quite confident about tomorrow afternoon's storm but much less certain about the weekend after next. It is also why the same app may change its prediction several times in a week. The forecast is not being careless; it is being updated with fresh information as the date gets closer.<br><br>Teachers in some schools now use forecasts to introduce probability in maths lessons. Students record the predicted chance of rain each morning and note whether it actually rained. After a month, they compare the two. The results usually show that the forecasts were far more reliable than the class expected.",
