  "title": "Is It Too Late to Learn the Guitar?",
  "topic": "music",
  "words": 311,
  "passage": "Ask most people when they should have started learning an instrument and they will sigh and say \"as a child\". There is some truth in this. Young children pick up rhythm and pitch easily, and their hands grow used to the shapes of chords before they know the names of the notes. But the idea that a teenager or an adult has missed the boat is simply wrong, and it stops thousands of people from doing something they would enjoy.<br><br>Research on learning suggests that older beginners have advantages of their own. A fifteen-year-old can read instructions, understand why a scale is built the way it is, and practise on purpose rather than by accident. Children often need a parent to remind them; a teenager who has chosen the guitar herself will find twenty minutes for it without being asked. Motivation, not age, is the best predictor of who will still be playing a year later.<br><br>What does hold older beginners back is embarrassment. A six-year-old does not mind sounding terrible; a sixteen-year-old minds very much, especially when a cousin has been playing since she was four. Teachers say the students who improve fastest are those who accept that the first month will sound bad and simply keep going. Recording yourself once a week, and listening back a month later, is one way to see progress that is invisible from day to day.<br><br>The other secret is to play music you actually like. Traditional lessons often begin with exercises that nobody would choose to hear, and beginners give up before they reach a real song. Learning three chords and a song you love in the first week does more for a new player than a term of scales. The scales can come later — and, oddly enough, they usually do, once the player wants to understand what she is already doing.",
