  "title": "Two Chairs by the Window",
  "topic": "friendship",
  "words": 317,
  "passage": "When my family moved to Da Nang, I was eleven and certain that I would never make another friend. My old school was five hundred kilometres away, and my best friend, Linh, had promised to write every week. She kept her promise for about a month. After that, the letters became shorter, and then they stopped. I did not blame her. She had a new class, a new football team and, I supposed, new friends.<br><br>The girl who sat next to me in my new school was called Mai. She did not say much at first. In fact, for the whole of the first term, our conversations were mostly about borrowing pens. Then one rainy afternoon, when the buses were late, we sat on two plastic chairs by the classroom window and talked for an hour. I discovered that she collected old stamps, that she was afraid of dogs, and that she had also moved schools, twice. By the end of the year, we were doing homework at each other's houses.<br><br>People sometimes say that a true friend is someone you can be silent with. I think that is only half the story. Mai and I could be silent together, but we could also argue, and the arguments never lasted. Once, we did not speak for three days after a disagreement about a school project. On the fourth day, she left a stamp from Brazil on my desk. Neither of us mentioned the argument again.<br><br>I am fifteen now, and Linh and I still send each other a message on our birthdays. I do not think of her as a lost friend, just a friend from another chapter. What the last four years have taught me is that friendship does not depend on how long you have known someone. It depends on whether both people are willing to sit down on those two chairs and keep talking.",
