  "level": "kc",
  "title": "Jobs That Did Not Have Names",
  "topic": "jobs",
  "words": 318,
  "passage": "Ask your grandparents what they wanted to be when they were fifteen, and you will probably hear a short list: teacher, doctor, farmer, soldier, shopkeeper. Ask a fifteen-year-old today and the list is longer and stranger. Some want to be game designers, drone pilots or online video makers. Twenty years ago, most of those jobs did not even have names.<br><br>Careers advisers say this change is happening faster than ever. A report from a Vietnamese employment website last year found that nearly a third of the jobs advertised in Ho Chi Minh City did not exist in the same form in 2010. Many of them are connected to technology, but not all. Delivery riders, for example, have become a common sight in every city, and their work depends on a phone app that was invented only a few years ago.<br><br>For students, this can sound worrying. How can you prepare for a job that has not been invented yet? Nguyen Thi Hoa, who advises secondary-school students in Hanoi, tells them not to panic. \"Nobody expects you to choose one job for life anymore,\" she says. \"Employers are looking for people who can learn quickly and work well with others. Those skills do not go out of date.\"<br><br>She also warns against following the crowd. When a job looks exciting on social media, thousands of young people rush towards it, and within a few years there are too many of them. Careers that look boring today, such as plumbing or nursing, may be the ones with the best pay and the most security in 2040.<br><br>Perhaps the best advice comes from Hoa's own story. She trained as an accountant, disliked it, worked in a bookshop for six years, and finally became a careers adviser at thirty-five. \"Every job I did taught me something I use now,\" she says. \"The path was not straight, but none of it was wasted.\"",
