  "id": "rd-ch-07-3",
  "level": "ch",
  "title": "Counting the Unseen",
  "topic": "wildlife conservation",
  "words": 526,
  "passage": "<b>A.</b> You cannot protect what you cannot count, and for most of the last century counting wild animals meant seeing them. Surveyors walked fixed lines through the bush noting every nest, tallied tracks in fresh snow, or flew over herds in light aircraft. For animals that live in the open this worked tolerably well. For the rest — a nocturnal cat in a mountain forest, a fish in a turbid river, a frog that calls for two nights a year — the published figures were often little more than guesses dressed up in decimals. More than one species declared extinct turned out simply to be one that nobody had looked for hard enough.<br><br><b>B.</b> The first tool to change this was almost absurdly simple: a camera strapped to a tree, triggered by movement and body heat. Left for weeks, it records whatever walks past, by day or by night, without a human within miles. Where animals carry individual markings — the stripes of a tiger, the rosettes of a leopard, the notched fin of a dolphin — each photograph can be matched to a known individual, and statisticians can estimate the whole population from how often familiar faces reappear. The results upended settled beliefs. A forest written off as empty of large predators proved to hold a breeding population; a reserve celebrated for its tigers held a fraction of the number printed in its brochure.<br><br><b>C.</b> The second tool does not need to see the animal at all. Every creature sheds skin cells, scales, mucus and waste into its surroundings, and a litre of river water, filtered and sequenced, yields a list of the species that have passed upstream in the previous few days. Environmental DNA, as the method is called, has found rare newts in ponds where a decade of netting had found nothing, and it can flag an invasive fish long before it is common enough to catch. Its limits are real, though. It says that a species is present, not how many there are, and because DNA drifts with the current, \"where\" is only approximate.<br><br><b>D.</b> Better numbers, it turns out, do not automatically produce better decisions. When a survey reveals fewer animals than everyone believed, funders may read the result as failure and withdraw; when it reveals more, a ministry may conclude that the species needs no protection after all and open the forest to logging. One field biologist told me, not entirely joking, that the most useful figure her organisation ever published was a slightly optimistic one. The new tools have made that kind of usefulness impossible.<br><br><b>E.</b> The next generation of methods is already in the field: microphones that log every call in a forest, software that sorts a million images in an afternoon, village volunteers who maintain the cameras and are paid for each verified sighting. What none of these can do is explain. A camera will tell you that a population has halved in five years; it will not tell you whether the cause is poaching, disease, a new road or a drought a hundred miles away. The count is where the work begins, not where it ends.",
