  "id": "rd-ch-08-1",
  "level": "ch",
  "title": "The Arithmetic of the Corner Shop",
  "topic": "economics of small business",
  "words": 542,
  "passage": "<b>A.</b> Ask most people why small shops close and they will reach for the obvious villain: the supermarket down the road, the online giant, the landlord who doubled the rent. All of these matter. Yet economists who study small enterprises point to a quieter culprit, one that rarely makes the news because it is not dramatic. It is the slow mismatch between what a business earns and when it earns it. A bakery may be perfectly profitable on paper and still fail, because its bills arrive on the first of the month while its customers pay in dribs and drabs over thirty days.<br><br><b>B.</b> This gap between profit and cash is the first thing that surprises new owners. Suppose a café sells a coffee for three pounds that costs one pound to make. The margin looks handsome. What the figure hides is everything that must be paid before the first cup is poured: the lease, the machine, the staff who stand ready through the empty hours between breakfast and lunch. Fixed costs, as they are called, do not shrink when custom is slow, and it is these, far more than the price of milk, that decide whether the shutters stay up. Only when the daily takings clear that fixed threshold does a single sale begin to add anything to the owner's pocket.<br><br><b>C.</b> No sooner has an owner grasped this than a second lesson arrives: scale cuts both ways. A chain can negotiate cheaper flour, spread the cost of an accountant across fifty branches, and survive a bad month in one town because a good month elsewhere covers it. A single shop has none of these cushions. On the other hand, the chain must standardise, and standardisation is precisely what a small business need not do. It can know its regulars by name, change the menu on a Tuesday because a supplier turned up with unexpected plums, and turn a passing enthusiasm into a product by Friday. Had the corner shop tried to compete on price, it would have lost years ago; that it competes on nimbleness is the reason it is still there.<br><br><b>D.</b> Then there is the question of what an owner's time is actually worth. Many sole traders never put their own wage into the accounts, so the business appears to make money when, in reality, it is being subsidised by seventy-hour weeks at a rate no employee would accept. Economists call this the opportunity cost — the salary the owner is quietly forgoing elsewhere — and its omission is the single most common reason a shop that \"does well\" leaves its owner poorer each year.<br><br><b>E.</b> None of this is an argument against opening a shop. It is an argument for opening one with the sums done honestly. The most durable small businesses tend to share three habits: they keep a reserve equal to at least two months of fixed costs, they price for the quiet weeks rather than the busy ones, and they treat the owner's time as an expense from day one. What they offer that no spreadsheet can capture is the reason customers walk past a cheaper alternative to reach them — and that, in the end, is the only margin that cannot be undercut.",
