  "id": "rd-ch-08-2",
  "level": "ch",
  "title": "Small Boxes, Long Journeys",
  "topic": "space exploration",
  "words": 505,
  "passage": "For most of the space age, a mission was an event. Years of design went into a single spacecraft, which was launched once, on a rocket that was thrown away, towards a target it would never be able to revisit. [1] The cost of failure was so enormous that engineers built in redundancy upon redundancy, which made every craft heavier, which made the rocket bigger, which made the whole enterprise dearer still.<br><br>That logic has begun to unwind. [2] A university department that could never have afforded a place on a rocket thirty years ago can today book a corner of a shared launch for less than the price of a laboratory instrument. What was once the preserve of a handful of national agencies is now open to start-ups, schools and, in a few cases, individuals.<br><br>The consequences for science are subtle rather than spectacular. A single large observatory can see farther than a swarm of small ones, and nobody expects a shoebox to photograph the rings of Saturn. [3] Twenty cheap satellites watching the same storm from twenty angles, or a dozen probes dropped into a comet's tail, produce a kind of knowledge that a lone, precious instrument cannot: knowledge of how things vary. Not until the price of a launch fell did it become possible to ask such questions at all.<br><br>There are costs, of course, and not only financial ones. Low orbit is filling up. [4] Astronomers complain that streaks of reflected sunlight now cross their long exposures, and the risk of collision rises with every launch. Whether the benefits of cheap access outweigh the clutter it creates is a question the field has only just started to take seriously.<br><br>What seems certain is that the age of the single, irreplaceable mission is passing. [5] It is the engineers who are learning to accept the occasional loss, and the scientists who are learning to think in populations rather than in individuals, who are shaping the next chapter. Had the old model persisted, the sky would be quieter — and a great deal less would be known about it.<br><br><i>Choose from the sentences A–G the one which fits each gap. There are two extra sentences you do not need to use.</i><br><br><b>A.</b> Yet there are questions that a single instrument, however powerful, is simply the wrong tool to answer.<br><b>B.</b> Its successor will not be one grander programme but a scattering of smaller, riskier and more frequent ones.<br><b>C.</b> The first human landing on another planet is now expected within the lifetime of today's students.<br><b>D.</b> Nothing could be repaired, replaced or retried once the countdown reached zero.<br><b>E.</b> Thousands of small satellites, many of them no longer working, now share a band of space that was almost empty two decades ago.<br><b>F.</b> Rockets whose first stages fly back and land have cut the price of reaching orbit dramatically, and satellites have shrunk to the size of a loaf of bread.<br><b>G.</b> Most of these instruments are assembled by the same three manufacturers.",
