  "title": "What Travelled With the Silk",
  "topic": "ancient trade routes",
  "words": 533,
  "passage": "<b>A.</b> The popular picture of an ancient trade route is a single caravan setting out from one end of a continent and, months later, arriving at the other. Almost no one made such a journey. Goods travelled far; people, on the whole, did not. A bale of cloth might pass through a dozen pairs of hands, each merchant carrying it only as far as the next market he knew, selling it on at a small profit and turning for home. The route was a relay, not a marathon, and its length was the sum of many short, familiar trips rather than one heroic expedition.<br><br><b>B.</b> What decided where those trips went was rarely the shortest line on a map. It was water. Caravans followed strings of oases across deserts, and the distance between one well and the next set the size of the load an animal could carry. Where rivers ran in the right direction, cargo left the road altogether. On the coasts, the seasonal winds mattered more than any harbour: a ship that missed the turning of the monsoon might wait half a year for the next one. The geography of trade, in other words, was written by rainfall and wind long before any ruler drew a border across it.<br><br><b>C.</b> Cities grew wherever a route was forced to pause. A mountain pass that closed in winter, a river that had to be crossed by ferry, a stretch of desert that could only be entered with a fresh supply of water — each created a place where goods piled up, where merchants waited, and where someone could make a living by feeding, lodging and taxing them. Some of these halts became great cities whose wealth depended entirely on the traffic passing through; when the traffic shifted, the city withered, sometimes within a single generation.<br><br><b>D.</b> The cargo that mattered most was frequently the cargo nobody had paid for. Along with the silk and the spices went the techniques for making paper, the method of casting a particular kind of metal, a new way of tuning a stringed instrument, and stories that surface, subtly altered, in the folk tales of peoples thousands of kilometres apart. Religions moved along the same paths, carried by merchants who prayed at each stop and by monks who found in the caravan the safest way to travel. So, less happily, did disease; a route that could carry a rumour from one end of a continent to the other could carry a plague just as efficiently.<br><br><b>E.</b> Why did the great overland routes fade? Not, as is often said, because ships were suddenly found to be faster; sailors had been carrying goods along the coasts for as long as caravans had crossed the interior. What changed was the balance of cost and risk. A single ship could carry the load of hundreds of animals and pass through the hands of one merchant rather than a dozen, and every pair of hands the cargo avoided was a toll, a bribe or a theft it also avoided. Once the sea became reliably cheaper, the halts along the old roads lost their reason to exist, and the relay quietly stopped being run.",
