  "passage": "<b>A.</b> For the best part of two decades, the annual publication of the national school rankings was treated as a fixture of the calendar, as predictable as the examinations whose results it digested. Little did the ministry that introduced the tables imagine that it would one day be the body to bury them. Yet that is precisely what happened last spring, when, without so much as a press release, the rankings vanished from the ministry's website. In their place appeared a searchable dashboard on which no school is placed above any other.<br><br><b>B.</b> It is worth recalling why the tables were created in the first place. Their champions argued that parents were choosing schools in the dark, and that a public ranking would let the sunlight in. Weak schools would be exposed; strong ones would be rewarded with applications; all of them would be pushed to improve. \"Transparency was the whole argument,\" recalls a former adviser who helped to draft the policy. \"And in a narrow sense it worked. Scores in the subjects we measured went up, year after year.\" She pauses before adding that narrowness was precisely the problem.<br><br><b>C.</b> What the tables measured, schools taught; what the tables ignored, schools quietly dropped. Art, music and outdoor education shrank to make room for examination practice. Some schools discovered that discouraging their weakest pupils from sitting a paper did more for their position than teaching them ever could. Nowhere was this more visible than in the crowded middle of the table, where a single percentage point could move a school twenty places in either direction, and where head teachers came to feel that they were managing a number rather than a community. The tables had begun as a description of schools; before long, schools had reshaped themselves around the tables.<br><br><b>D.</b> The dashboard that replaced them offers a deliberately untidy picture. Alongside examination results sit measures of pupils' progress since entry, attendance, staff turnover and the findings of an annual wellbeing survey. No composite score is calculated, and the ministry has stated that it has no intention of calculating one. Critics call the result opaque. Parents seem divided: in a survey published last month, roughly a third said they missed having \"a simple answer\", but fewer than one in ten said they would now choose a different school for their child.<br><br><b>E.</b> Whether the reform succeeds will not be known for years, and perhaps not even then, since the question of what makes a good school has never been settled. What the episode does reveal is something about policy itself. A measure that begins as a description tends, once it carries consequences, to become a target, and a target, sooner or later, ceases to describe. Had the ministry foreseen this, it might have built its tables differently. It remains to be seen whether the dashboard, should parents ever come to lean on it as heavily, can escape the same fate.",
