  "title": "Why Forgetting Is Not a Failure",
  "topic": "neuroscience of memory",
  "words": 481,
  "passage": "Ask most people what a good memory is and they will describe a kind of vault: things go in, stay put, and come out unchanged on demand. [1] The brain, on the evidence of the last thirty years, is less a vault than an editor, forever revising the manuscript it holds.<br><br>Consider what happens in the hours after learning. A new experience is first held in a fragile form that depends heavily on the hippocampus, a curved structure deep inside the temporal lobe. [2] During this period, and above all during deep, slow-wave sleep, the hippocampus appears to replay the day's patterns to the cortex at high speed, gradually handing over responsibility for the memory. Rats that have learned a maze show, while asleep, the very same sequences of neural firing that they showed while running it, only compressed, as though the recording were being played back on fast-forward.<br><br>What is striking is that this hand-over is never quite complete. Each time a memory is recalled, it seems to return briefly to a fragile state, open to alteration before it is stored once more. [3] The phenomenon, known as reconsolidation, goes a long way towards explaining why an eyewitness can come to remember, in perfect sincerity, details she was only told afterwards, and why a family story retold across three generations drifts so far from the events that gave rise to it.<br><br>Forgetting, too, turns out to be a far more active business than the vault picture allows. [4] Researchers have since identified cells in the hippocampus whose job appears to be exactly this: to weaken connections that are no longer being used, clearing space for the patterns that matter. Far from being a leak in the system, a certain amount of forgetting is the system working as intended.<br><br>None of this means that memory is unreliable in any everyday sense. [5] Rather, it suggests that the vault was always the wrong metaphor. What we carry is not a record of the past but a working model of it, tuned during sleep, revised at every retrieval and pruned continuously; an account that is generally good enough and occasionally, quietly, wrong.<br><br><b>A.</b> Were it merely a matter of decay, old memories would all fade at roughly the same rate, which they plainly do not.<br><b>B.</b> Anyone who has caught a cherished childhood memory in a mistake already knows how misleading that picture can be.<br><b>C.</b> The cortex, by contrast, is thought to hold very little of what it receives for longer than a few seconds.<br><b>D.</b> Over the following nights, however, something remarkable takes place.<br><b>E.</b> Most of what we remember about yesterday is, after all, broadly accurate.<br><b>F.</b> New information encountered at that moment can be folded into the original, so that the next recollection contains both.<br><b>G.</b> Such experiments, however, have so far been carried out only on human volunteers.",
