  "topic": "architecture",
  "words": 484,
  "passage": "<b>A.</b> On the northern edge of a coastal city stands a row of houses that, at first sight, looks abandoned halfway through construction. Each has a concrete frame, a roof, a staircase, a kitchen and a bathroom, and beside each of these sits an empty void of exactly the same size, open to the sky. Nothing has gone wrong here. The void is the design. It was left, deliberately and at some cost to the architects' reputation, for the families themselves to fill.<br><br><b>B.</b> The reasoning was brutally simple. The public subsidy available for each family would buy either a small, complete house on cheap land an hour from the city, or half of a much better house in the neighbourhood where the families already lived and worked. Rather than build the whole of something poor, the architects chose to build the difficult half of something good: the structure, the plumbing, the party walls and the roof, the parts that a family with limited tools and no engineering training cannot safely construct alone. \"We did not design a house,\" the lead architect said at the time. \"We designed the hard half and got out of the way.\"<br><br><b>C.</b> A decade on, no two houses in the row are alike. Some voids have been filled in brick, some in timber, one in a patchwork of salvaged windows. One family has built outwards to open a small shop; another has added a second storey for a married daughter. Property values along the row have risen steadily, whereas a conventional, fully finished social-housing estate a few streets away has lost value over the same period. Not every outcome is happy, though. A handful of the extensions are poorly built, two families never completed theirs, and the row has lost the visual unity that architects are trained to prize.<br><br><b>D.</b> It was this last point that split the profession. To some, leaving half a house unbuilt was an abdication: handing families a problem and calling it participation. To others, self-building is simply what poorer households have always done, on hillsides and in back yards, and the scheme merely gave that instinct a safe skeleton to grow on. What the argument really turned on was not craftsmanship but the older question of who architecture is for, and whether the architect's vision should outrank the occupant's.<br><br><b>E.</b> The model has since been copied elsewhere, with mixed results. Where funding rules permit it, incremental schemes have flourished; where a subsidy may be spent only on a dwelling certified as complete, the approach is impossible. Yet the row's deepest challenge is not to housing policy but to the idea of completion itself. Buildings are normally at their best on the day they are handed over and decline from there. These houses invert that curve: at hand-over they are at their least finished, and, if their architects are right, at their most useful.",
