  "title": "Traffic in the Twilight Zone",
  "topic": "ocean science",
  "words": 531,
  "passage": "<b>A.</b> Ask most people to picture the deep ocean and they will describe a silent, lightless plain where nothing much happens. It is an understandable picture, and a wrong one. Below about two hundred metres, where sunlight fades to a faint blue and then to nothing, lies the largest habitat on the planet by volume, and it is neither quiet nor still. Marine scientists now describe this so-called twilight zone as a place of constant traffic, and one whose rhythms may matter for the climate of the entire globe.<br><br><b>B.</b> The traffic in question is a daily migration on a scale that dwarfs anything on land. Each evening, uncountable numbers of small fish, squid and shrimp-like creatures rise hundreds of metres towards the surface to feed under cover of darkness; each dawn they sink back into the gloom to hide from predators. Ship-mounted echo sounders first recorded this movement decades ago, when operators noticed a \"false seabed\" on their screens that rose at dusk and fell at daybreak. Only much later did anyone appreciate what the moving layer was made of, or how much carbon it carried down with it.<br><br><b>C.</b> That last point is what has drawn climate researchers to a zone they once ignored. Animals that feed near the surface and then descend are, in effect, a biological pump: the carbon in their food travels down inside their bodies and is released, through breathing and waste, at depths from which it may not return to the atmosphere for centuries. Estimates of how much carbon moves this way vary wildly, and therein lies the problem. Were the figure at the upper end of current guesses, the twilight zone would be storing more carbon each year than every forest on Earth combined; were it at the lower end, it would be a modest contributor at best. Nobody can yet say which.<br><br><b>D.</b> Closing that gap is harder than it sounds. Nets tear the soft-bodied animals apart or let them escape; cameras need light, which frightens off the very creatures they are meant to observe; and sound-based surveys cannot easily tell a shoal of fish from a cloud of jellyfish. One response has been to release fleets of small autonomous floats that drift with the currents for months at a time, recording temperature, oxygen and the faint acoustic signature of life around them. Another has been to listen in a different sense: hydrophones lowered into the darkness have picked up a low, rhythmic hum at dusk that some researchers believe is the collective sound of millions of animals beginning their nightly climb.<br><br><b>E.</b> All of this is unfolding just as commercial interest in the same waters is growing. Several fishing companies have begun to look at the twilight zone's small, oily fish as raw material for fishmeal and nutritional supplements, and a handful of governments have issued exploratory licences. Scientists have not, on the whole, called for a ban. What they have asked for is time: a pause long enough to learn what the zone does for the rest of the planet before anyone starts to remove pieces of it. Whether that request will be heard is, for now, an open question.",
