  "passage": "<b>A.</b> Every profession has a bottom rung, and for most of history that rung has been where the learning happened. The junior lawyer who spends a year checking contracts for a single clause, the trainee radiologist who reads a thousand routine scans before seeing a rare one, the graduate analyst who builds the same spreadsheet a hundred times: none of them enjoys the work, and all of them are being trained by it. The dull tasks at the bottom of a career are not an accident of bad organisation. They are how expertise is passed on.<br><br><b>B.</b> It is precisely these tasks that the current generation of artificial intelligence performs best. Software that summarises documents, drafts routine letters, flags anomalies in images and writes first-pass code is not replacing the expert at the top of the ladder, whose judgement it cannot yet match. It is replacing the apprentice at the bottom, whose work was always somewhat mechanical. Firms that have adopted such tools report exactly what one would expect: fewer junior hires, faster turnaround, and senior staff freed to concentrate on the parts of the job that genuinely require them.<br><br><b>C.</b> What those same firms have been slower to notice is the debt they are running up. If nobody reads the thousand routine scans, nobody develops the eye that spots the thousand-and-first. Had the tools existed a generation ago, the very people now supervising them would never have acquired the judgement that makes their supervision valuable. The problem is not that machines will take the senior jobs; it is that, a decade from now, there may be no one qualified to hold them. Economists have a name for a resource that is consumed faster than it is renewed, and nothing about the label is reassuring.<br><br><b>D.</b> Not everyone accepts this gloomy account. Sceptics point out that every wave of automation has provoked the same alarm, and that the professions have survived the calculator, the search engine and the spreadsheet. Skills, they argue, do not vanish; they migrate. A junior who no longer checks contracts by hand will instead learn to interrogate the machine's checking, to notice where it is confidently wrong, and to take responsibility for a judgement she did not personally form. That, they say, is a higher skill than the one it replaces, not a lower one.<br><br><b>E.</b> Both sides may be right, which is what makes the question so uncomfortable. It is entirely possible that the skill of supervising machines is genuinely valuable and that it cannot be learned without first doing, at least for a while, the work the machines now do. If so, the firms that will thrive are not those that cut the bottom rung fastest, but those that deliberately keep some of the drudgery in place and pay for it, knowing full well that a machine could do it more cheaply. Whether shareholders will tolerate such an arrangement is a question no algorithm can answer.",
